Install the bundled MariaDB dev plugin, update extensions with auto-update

The extension ships MariaDB's dev plugin (github.com/mariadb/ai-plugins,
claude/dev-plugin at the tag in mariadb/AI_PLUGINS), which
mariadb/build-vsix.sh puts into dist/mariadb-dev-plugin. On a start where
that plugin's version differs from the one recorded last (globalState
mariadbDevPluginVersion), the extension copies it to
<globalStorage>/mariadb-dev-plugin, a source that outlives the extension
version, and with the bundled CLI:
- installs it from there (`extensions install <copy> --consent`) when no dev
  extension is installed;
- updates the copy installed from there in place (`extensions update dev
  --consent`), which keeps the user's preferences and enablement for it;
- replaces the copy 26.11.2 installed from https://github.com/mariadb/ai-plugins
  (`extensions uninstall dev`, then install), once.
A dev extension from elsewhere (a link, another source) is left alone, and a
plugin the user uninstalled stays uninstalled (also when 26.11.2 recorded
mariadbDevPluginInstalled). The version is recorded only when the installed
plugin has it, so a failure (also one `extensions update` only reports on
stderr) is logged and retried on the next start; windows starting at once
then just update an up-to-date copy.

The update check of a local Claude plugin directory (originSource Claude, no
pluginName) converts it first, as for a Qoder plugin; it loaded the raw
directory, found no qwen-extension.json and reported the plugin as not
updatable, so `extensions update` never updated it.

On every start the extension then runs `extensions update <name> --consent`
for each user extension whose install metadata has autoUpdate: true (as the
terminal UI does at startup). `extensions update` gets --consent for that,
like `extensions install`. Failures are logged and retried on the next start.

All of it runs in the background; the daemon and the ACP process wait for it
before they start, so they load the installed or updated plugin. The setting
qwen-code.installMariaDbPlugin (on by default) turns off install and updates.

